{
  "description": "issue #766, review-gate regression: an addressing form reached from a per-turtle command's ARGUMENT. `forward nudge` runs once per addressed turtle (spec/turtles-and-sprites.md:113) and evaluates `nudge` inside each run, so the `ask :t [ … ]` in `nudge`'s body (`:t` bound to `:a` at the call site) emits its entry/restore addressing events while the runtime is mid-way through the per-turtle loop. Two invariants are pinned here that top-level `tell`/`ask`/`each` fixtures cannot reach. (1) No addressing event carries an envelope `turtle_id`: the runtime stamps the acting turtle's id onto the turtle-specific events a per-turtle command run produces, and argument evaluation happens inside that window — but an addressing event describes a SET, so it is not turtle-specific (spec/execution-model.md:1005) and a stamp could name a turtle its own addressed set does not contain. (2) `current_turtle_id` is always the addressed set's first member, never the per-turtle loop's transient current-turtle pointer: during turtle 2's iteration that pointer is 2, yet the `ask`'s restore correctly reports `{ addressed_turtle_ids: [1, 2], current_turtle_id: 1 }`. The `right 1` turns carry turtle 1's id both times because `ask :a` addressed turtle 1, while `forward`'s own `move`/`draw-segment` pairs carry 1 then 2; the surrounding `instruction`/`procedure-enter`/`return`/`procedure-exit` events carry none, being program events rather than turtle events (issue #764). Issue #1099/#824: the turtle handle reaches the procedure as a PARAMETER rather than as a top-level name its body reads, because the sealed procedure boundary (spec/execution-model.md:389-394) makes that read `ol-var-not-visible`. This is an ADDRESSING fixture, not a scoping one, and the migration was measured to keep it so: the only change to the stream is that `procedure-enter`'s `args` now carries the handle, while every addressing, movement, print, and restore event stays byte-identical.",
